  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "/v/hiJR7QSNi6os8Ff6hYfpSdB4=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-13T13:59:09.228Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "## One engine, two faces, one package\n\nThe Powerhouse stack is a **local-first, event-sourced application platform**. Everything below is built on one idea: state is never written directly \u2014 it is derived by replaying an immutable log of typed operations.\n\n## Core engine\n\n- **Reactor** \u2014 runs reducers, appends operations to storage, drives processors, and syncs with peer Reactors over the DocSync protocol. Storage-agnostic and modular; loads document models at runtime.\n- **Operation sync, not state sync** \u2014 offline edits replay on the remote Reactor, so both ends converge without last-write-wins or manual merges.\n- **Pluggable operation stores** \u2014 including a Hypercore-backed store (`reactor-hypercore`) alongside the default.\n\n## Host applications\n\n- **Connect** \u2014 browser collaboration workspace embedding a Reactor, so it works offline and syncs on reconnect. Renders your editors and drive-apps.\n- **Switchboard** \u2014 server host exposing the Reactor over **GraphQL** and **MCP**. Runs processors and maintains read models.\n- **Vetra** \u2014 the agent-first builder platform and local dev environment (Vetra Studio + Vetra Switchboard), spec-driven and AI-native.\n- **Fusion** and **Renown** round out the four host applications.\n\n## What builders ship\n\nA single **package** bundles document models (schemas + reducers), editors, processors, and subgraphs, and plugs into all three layers at once: the Reactor runs the reducers, Connect renders the editors, Switchboard runs the processors.\n\n## Feature surface by area\n\n| Area | What the stack offers |\n|---|---|\n| Modelling | GraphQL-defined document models with typed operations, scoped state (global/local), and versioning/migration support |\n| Codegen | `codegen` generates TypeScript types, reducers, and editor scaffolding from the GraphQL spec |\n| API | `reactor-api` auto-generates a GraphQL subgraph per document model, plus the core reactor subgraph |\n| Read models | Processors fold the operation stream into queryable projections; relational processors are backed by PGlite |\n| Analytics | `analytics-engine` for time-series/dimensional analysis over document data |\n| Client | `reactor-browser` provides React hooks and `IReactorClient` for editors and drive-apps |\n| Identity | `renown` \u2014 wallet-based authentication and identity; document permissions, signing, and subgraph-level authorization |\n| Attachments | `reactor-attachments` \u2014 content-addressed storage that keeps large binaries out of the action pipeline |\n| Groups | `reactor-group` \u2014 group membership referenced by `{ group }` principals in the auth scope |\n| AI integration | `reactor-mcp` exposes document model operations and drive management to AI coding agents over MCP |\n| Design system | `design-system` / document-engineering \u2014 GraphQL scalar UI components |\n| Tooling | `ph-cli` / `ph-cmd`, `builder-tools` (Vite + TS config, dev server), `registry` for package dependency resolution |\n\n## Version context\n\nDescribes the stack at **v6.2.2-dev.45** (2026-08-13). Most other Powerhouse knowledge in this vault is baselined at v6.0.0-dev.254 \u2014 see the corpus baseline note.\n",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-13T13:59:01.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-13T13:59:09.228Z"
    }
  },
